<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Green Mountain Boys</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1770s</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Green Mountain Boys were a militia formed around 1770 by settlers holding land grants from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-hampshire">New Hampshire</a> in what is now Vermont, to resist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>'s claims to the area. Led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethan-allen">Ethan Allen</a>, they drove off New York officials and surveyors. In May 1775, with Benedict Arnold, they captured Fort Ticonderoga from the British, and a regiment of that name served in the American War of Independence.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/green-mountain-boys.jpg" alt="Green Mountain Boys" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mysid at en.wikipedia, revised version first uploaded by Lexicon. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Vermont_Republic.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Green Mountain Boys arose in what is now south-western Vermont among settlers and land speculators holding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-hampshire">New Hampshire</a> titles to land between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/connecticut-river">Connecticut River</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-champlain">Lake Champlain</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-hampshire-grants">New Hampshire Grants</a>. A decision of the British crown had given <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> legal control of the area, and New York refused to honour those titles and town charters. A few towns with New York titles, notably Brattleboro, accepted the change, but most settlers of the thinly peopled frontier rejected New York's authority.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>With several hundred members, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethan-allen">Ethan Allen</a>, his brother Ira Allen and their cousins Seth Warner and Remember Baker, and based at the Catamount Tavern in Bennington, the Boys became an armed force and de facto government. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>'s arrest warrants against their leaders could not be served. Its surveyors and officials were turned back and some badly beaten, and settlers under New York grants were driven off. After two men were killed when anti-Yorkers occupied the courthouse at Westminster, Allen led a band there and organised a convention calling for independence from New York.</p><p>In 1775 Allen and his men, with Colonel Benedict Arnold of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/connecticut">Connecticut</a>, also took Crown Point and Fort George, and briefly held St. John's in Québec. That summer the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/continental-congress">Continental Congress</a> authorised a ranger regiment from the Grants, which chose Seth Warner as colonel and fought at Hubbardton and Bennington in 1777. Some who stayed with Allen were captured with him in August 1775 in a failed attempt on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montreal">Montreal</a>, among them Matthew Lyon. Vermont declared itself independent in January 1777 and based its armed forces on the Boys; during the Haldimand Affair some members negotiated secretly with British officials about restoring the Crown's rule.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Several members went on to lead the new state: Ira Allen founded the University of Vermont, Jonas Galusha became governor, and Moses Robinson was governor of the Vermont Republic and one of the state's first two senators. Vermont joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> as the 14th state in 1791. The Bennington Museum holds the remnant of a Green Mountain Boys flag believed to have belonged to John Stark, one of the few surviving regimental flags of the Revolution. The Vermont National Guard is still known as the Green Mountain Boys and uses the original battle flag as its banner.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Green Mountain Boys” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Settlers holding New Hampshire grants resisted New York through Ethan Allen's <b>Green Mountain Boys</b>, and in 1777 conventions at Westminster and Windsor declared an independent state, first called New Connecticut and then Vermont.</blockquote><a href="/state/vermont-republic">Vermont Republic</a></li></ul></section>
